1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,660
Companies run on data pipelines.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:09,140
Raw tables feed cleaned tables, which
feed the dashboards executives read.

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:16,115
DataHub is an open source catalog that
maps every table, every column, and

4
00:00:16,135 --> 00:00:18,075
every dependency between them.

5
00:00:18,535 --> 00:00:22,315
DataHub also stores what you
need when something breaks:

6
00:00:22,855 --> 00:00:27,515
schemas, owners, usage,
incidents, assertions.

7
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:34,300
Six read tools reach it through
DataHub's MCP server; seventeen

8
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:39,180
more, on the Python SDK, run the
deeper checks and write back.

9
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:41,980
This is the agent's control panel.

10
00:00:42,365 --> 00:00:45,665
On the right, the catalog
snapshot from DataHub:

11
00:00:46,445 --> 00:00:51,185
twenty three assets in a ride
hailing data warehouse, all healthy.

12
00:00:51,565 --> 00:00:54,065
Now we stop the ingestion job.

13
00:00:54,780 --> 00:01:00,480
An assertion is an automated data quality
check DataHub runs against a table.

14
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:07,580
Downstream checks start failing, and the
inbox on the left fills up. The top row:

15
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:13,505
agg daily rides has collapsed to
four rows, where its check expected

16
00:01:13,565 --> 00:01:15,025
at least twenty five.

17
00:01:15,565 --> 00:01:18,545
One click, and the agent takes the page.

18
00:01:19,085 --> 00:01:20,865
Its first move is memory.

19
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:26,920
It searches DataHub for post mortems it
wrote before, indexed as a searchable

20
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,320
structured property. Nothing.

21
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:36,105
So it climbs the lineage, the map of which
table feeds which, one hop at a time.

22
00:01:36,505 --> 00:01:40,525
At every table it asks what a
human would, all through DataHub.

23
00:01:40,985 --> 00:01:42,525
Is the assertion passing.

24
00:01:42,825 --> 00:01:44,205
Is the data fresh.

25
00:01:44,425 --> 00:01:45,965
Did the row count collapse.

26
00:01:46,265 --> 00:01:47,565
Did the schema change.

27
00:01:48,265 --> 00:01:53,880
Staging trips is broken, but its parent
is worse, so it keeps climbing and

28
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:55,680
stops at trips raw.

29
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:59,600
Twenty six hours stale
against a six hour limit.

30
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:03,680
Nothing feeds it, and it confirms
that rather than assuming.

31
00:02:04,325 --> 00:02:05,785
Then it works downstream:

32
00:02:06,485 --> 00:02:09,945
fifteen assets, ranked
by usage from DataHub.

33
00:02:10,645 --> 00:02:16,545
Then it acts inside DataHub, filing
an incident, tagging the root cause

34
00:02:16,565 --> 00:02:22,150
down to the column, notifying the owners,
and writing a post mortem back in.

35
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:25,830
Now a different table
breaks, three hops away.

36
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:28,150
This time the search hits.

37
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:34,405
It finds its own post mortem, then uses
DataHub's column level lineage to check

38
00:02:34,425 --> 00:02:38,205
the path from the same source,
and goes straight to the answer.

39
00:02:38,745 --> 00:02:40,125
Same root cause.

40
00:02:40,505 --> 00:02:41,885
On this pair of runs:

41
00:02:42,105 --> 00:02:47,925
twenty four percent fewer tool calls,
fourteen percent less time, the

42
00:02:47,945 --> 00:02:49,325
same three hops.

43
00:02:50,246 --> 00:02:54,160
Written back into DataHub, where
the next engineer finds it.
